+# Week 7 Rank 8 Competitor and Fact Check
+
+## Target
+
+- Primary keyword: `gst on gold jewellery`
+- Target slug: `gst-on-gold-jewellery-2026`
+- Competitor: https://www.caratlane.com/blog/how-much-gst-is-applied-to-gold-jewellery-in-india/
+- Checked: 2026-08-19
+
+## Competitor structure
+
+The supplied page uses one H1, a short definition, a pre and post GST comparison, one worked 10 gram chain example, embedded product promotions, related design links, and no visible FAQ section. The page addresses the main rate and buyer calculation intent, but it does not provide a clear current invoice checklist, intra-state and inter-state tax split, or a sourced explanation that separates a retail jewellery sale from upstream job work.
+
+## Differentiation plan
+
+1. Lead with the current official answer and the total transaction value rule.
+2. Explain why a retail buyer should not add 3% on metal plus another 5% on making charges.
+3. Show a reusable formula and clearly labelled hypothetical calculations.
+4. Separate CGST plus SGST from IGST without changing the total rate.
+5. Explain old gold exchange treatment using the CBIC FAQ.
+6. Add a practical invoice checklist using BIS consumer guidance.
+7. Add visible FAQs and FAQPage schema based on the relevant Week 7 keyword map.
+
+## Current official facts
+
+- CBIC lists articles of jewellery under HSN 7113 at 1.5% central tax plus 1.5% state or union territory tax, or 3% integrated tax.
+- CBIC's gems and jewellery sectoral FAQ says 3% applies to the total transaction value of jewellery whether making charges are shown separately or not.
+- The same sectoral FAQ says 5% applies to a registered job worker's job charges. This is an upstream business treatment, not an extra retail levy to add to the customer's jewellery transaction value.
+- CBIC's general FAQ says that when old gold is exchanged while buying a new 20 gram set, GST is charged on the open market value of the entire 20 gram set under the stated valuation rule.
+- BIS says a hallmarked jewellery invoice should state the article description, net precious-metal weight, purity in carat and fineness, and hallmarking charges. It also tells consumers they can verify purity through a BIS recognized Assaying and Hallmarking centre.
+
+## Authoritative sources
+
+- CBIC sectoral FAQ: https://cbic-gst.gov.in/hindi/sectoral-faq.html
+- CBIC GST goods and services rates: https://cbic-gst.gov.in/hindi/gst-goods-services-rates.html
+- CBIC general FAQ: https://cbic-gst.gov.in/faq.html
+- BIS jeweller and invoice guidance: https://www.bis.gov.in/hallmarking-jewellers/?lang=en
+
+## Editorial caution
+
+The article will state that tax rules and invoice treatment can change and that buyers should check the current tax invoice or obtain professional advice for unusual transactions. It will not copy competitor wording or reuse the competitor's numeric example.
